-- examples/checks/agg-minimax-demo-check.rofl — agg_minimax_demo, TIC-TAC-TOE
-- BY MINIMAX (examples/minimax/README.md). The rules are
-- examples/minimax/minimax.rofl; the world has no data file, the game is the
-- rules.
--
-- BY HAND: the game is a draw, and every opening is a draw (nine rows at 0
-- in v1). After a corner, O must answer in the centre: a reply on an edge or
-- on a corner loses (7 of the 8 replies are 1 for X, the centre is 0). With X
-- on the first row's two left cells and O in the centre and O to move, one
-- move keeps the draw, the block at the third cell, and the other five
-- lose. With X on a corner and the centre and O on an edge and the far corner,
-- X to move, two moves win (cells 4 and 7) and three do not. 5 478 boards are real; the last ply has 78 of them, 62 won by X, 16
-- drawn and none won by O (O has only four marks).
--
-- The oracle is a script of its own, a memoised minimax over tuples in Python
-- that shares nothing with these rules: per ply how many boards are real and
-- how many are worth -1, 0 and 1, and the value of every board of plies 1
-- and 2. mm_ply(Ply, Boards, OWins, Draws, XWins).
mm_ply(0, 1, 0, 1, 0).
mm_ply(1, 9, 0, 9, 0).
mm_ply(2, 72, 0, 24, 48).
mm_ply(3, 252, 50, 138, 64).
mm_ply(4, 756, 36, 136, 584).
mm_ply(5, 1260, 540, 264, 456).
mm_ply(6, 1520, 264, 200, 1056).
mm_ply(7, 1140, 416, 200, 524).
mm_ply(8, 390, 168, 80, 142).
mm_ply(9, 78, 0, 16, 62).
mm_game(0).
mm_val(1, b(e,e,e,e,e,e,e,e,x), 0).
mm_val(1, b(e,e,e,e,e,e,e,x,e), 0).
mm_val(1, b(e,e,e,e,e,e,x,e,e), 0).
mm_val(1, b(e,e,e,e,e,x,e,e,e), 0).
